# What NOT to ship to nl-hta-navigator-dev-func.
#
# function_app.py is self-contained: it imports no sibling module and the only
# file it opens at runtime is config.toml (see CONFIG_PATH, line 38). So the
# deployment needs exactly four files -- function_app.py, config.toml,
# host.json, requirements.txt -- and everything else here is local tooling.
#
# config.toml is deliberately NOT excluded. The function app has no NEO4J_* or
# BEDROCK_* app settings in Azure; it reads all of them from this file. Exclude
# it and every endpoint deploys cleanly and then fails at the Neo4j driver.

# Version control. There is a 22 MB repo here; none of it belongs on the host.
.git
.gitignore

# Virtualenvs and caches
.venv
__pycache__
*.pyc

# Oryx writes this on the server during a remote build. Shipping a local copy
# can collide with what the build produces.
.python_packages

# Ingest toolchain and its ~1.7 GB mirrored PDF corpus. Excluding this is the
# difference between a 200 KB upload and a failed multi-gigabyte one.
Dev/

# Node artifacts. This is a Python app; nothing here is used.
node_modules
package.json
package-lock.json

# Superseded copies of code and config. The config backups carry credentials
# that have since been replaced -- there is no reason to put them on a server.
*bkp*
*bak*
*-bk_*
config_now.toml
old_scripts-bkp_2209/
working_backendcode_1108

# Standalone ingest and discovery scripts, run by hand and never imported.
run_pipeline.py
run_diagnostics.py
zin_*.py
autoingestionfromzin-url/

# Local-only settings and scratch data
local.settings.json
log.txt
probe.txt
neo4j_poc_diagnostics.json
*.jsonl
zin_data1/
zin_data_old/
zin_pdfs/
